From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nl80211: end the wait for a reply that never comes

send_and_recv_glb() reads replies while err.err is positive. The kernel
queues its answer while it handles the request, but drops it when the
receive queue of the socket is full at that moment (the next read then
fails with ENOBUFS). The loop then waits for a reply that never comes.
On a socket that an eloop handler also reads, such as the nl_connect
socket of a BSS, which gets the EAPOL frames of its stations and sends
SET_BEACON, the socket is non-blocking: nl_recvmsgs() returns 0 when
there is nothing to read, and the loop spins at full CPU instead.
Either way the event loop stops, and with it every station, control and
ubus interface, until hostapd is restarted.

Wait for each part of the reply with poll(), and end the request with
-ETIMEDOUT after 5 seconds without one. The kernel has handled the
request by the time it was sent, and each part of a dump follows the
read before it, so a reply that is seconds late was lost.

Signed-off-by: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c | 34 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c b/src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c
index 2c7a2d8..4da8f8f 100644
--- a/src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c
+++ b/src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/utsname.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
+#include <poll.h>
 #include <net/if.h>
 #include <netlink/genl/genl.h>
 #include <netlink/genl/ctrl.h>
@@ -519,6 +520,9 @@ out:
 }
 
 
+/* the longest wait for the next message of a reply after the request went out */
+#define NL80211_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS 5000
+
 int send_and_recv_glb(struct nl80211_global *global,
 		      struct wpa_driver_nl80211_data *drv, /* may be NULL */
 		      struct nl_sock *nl_handle, struct nl_msg *msg,
@@ -596,8 +600,36 @@ int send_and_recv_glb(struct nl80211_global *global,
 			  valid_handler, valid_data);
 
 	while (err.err > 0) {
-		int res = nl_recvmsgs(nl_handle, cb);
+		struct pollfd pfd = {
+			.fd = nl_socket_get_fd(nl_handle),
+			.events = POLLIN,
+		};
+		int res;
+
+		/*
+		 * The kernel queues the reply while it handles the request, so
+		 * it is there unless the receive queue was full and the reply
+		 * was dropped (ENOBUFS). Don't wait for ever then, and don't
+		 * spin on a non-blocking socket (one that an eloop handler
+		 * reads, which also gets events and EAPOL frames), where
+		 * nl_recvmsgs() returns 0 when there is nothing to read.
+		 */
+		res = poll(&pfd, 1, NL80211_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS);
+		if (res < 0 && errno == EINTR)
+			continue;
+		if (res <= 0) {
+			wpa_printf(MSG_ERROR,
+				   "nl80211: no reply to command %u in %d ms%s%s",
+				   ((struct genlmsghdr *)
+				    nlmsg_data(nlmsg_hdr(msg)))->cmd,
+				   NL80211_REPLY_TIMEOUT_MS,
+				   res < 0 ? ": " : "",
+				   res < 0 ? strerror(errno) : "");
+			err.err = res < 0 ? -errno : -ETIMEDOUT;
+			break;
+		}
 
+		res = nl_recvmsgs(nl_handle, cb);
 		if (res == -NLE_DUMP_INTR) {
 			/* Most likely one of the nl80211 dump routines hit a
 			 * case where internal results changed while the dump
